#N canvas 40 60 1280 1140 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 21\ \ \ Genetic\ algorithms\ \ -\ \ breed\ feedback\ synths\ with\ your\ ears\ (1975\ /\ 1986) 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1110 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1110 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1110 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 Eight little feedback synths - four neurons each - form a population. You listen and rate them \, and a genetic algorithm breeds the next generation from your favourites: selection \, crossover and mutation of their weights and biases., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 1004 empty empty THE\ IDEA:\ evolution\ as\ a\ search 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 Learning does not need gradients. A GENETIC ALGORITHM (Holland \, 1975) keeps a POPULATION of candidate solutions \, scores each one (its FITNESS) and breeds the next generation from the better ones: SELECTION \, CROSSOVER \, MUTATION. Generation after generation the population climbs towards whatever the fitness rewards (Rojas \, chapter 17)., f 52;
#X text 20 248 HERE each candidate is a little feedback synth: four neurons that feed each other \, computed sample by sample - a CTRNN (lesson 9). Its GENOME is its 16 weights and 4 biases \, twenty numbers. Some genomes sing \, buzz or stutter \, others fall silent (the network settles into a fixed point)., f 52;
#X text 20 359 THE FITNESS IS YOUR EAR. Rojas: the function to optimise need not be a formula - run the experiment and score the result. Dawkins bred his biomorphs this way (1986) \, Karl Sims evolved images (1991) \, Beer and Gallagher evolved CTRNNs that make insects walk (1992)., f 52;
#X text 20 455 SELECTION: a parent is drawn with probability rating / (sum of all ratings) - a roulette wheel. Zero stars never breed. ELITISM copies the best card unchanged into card 1 \, so your favourite survives., f 52;
#X text 20 536 CROSSOVER: the child takes the first rows (neurons) from parent A and the rest from parent B. The cut falls only between neurons: the weights into one neuron work as a team (Rojas 17.3.1). Even so a child can fall silent - a neuron from one parent expects partners from its own family. Rojas calls it the problem of symmetries., f 52;
#X text 20 662 MUTATION: each gene is nudged \, with probability p \, by a random Gaussian step of size sigma. High p explores \, low p fine-tunes. Without mutation the population can only reshuffle what it has and drifts into copies of itself (genetic drift \, Rojas 17.2.3)., f 52;
#X text 20 758 A NEW START draws circuits the way Randall Beer built oscillators: every neuron excites itself \, every pair pushes and pulls (w_ij = -w_ji). Most of them sing - evolution takes it from there., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 860 414 empty empty THE\ POPULATION\ \ (8\ genomes\ =\ 8\ synths) 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
#X text 426 120 Each card is one genome. Its 4 rows are the 4 neurons \, a row holds the weights INTO that neuron from neurons 1 \, 2 \, 3 \, 4 and then its bias. Red = excites \, blue = inhibits \, white = nothing., f 118;
