#X obj 410 870 cnv 1 860 230 empty empty TRY\ THIS 8 12 0 13 #e6f6e9 #1d3557 0;
#X text 430 896 1) Raise the volume. Click listen on each card and rate it (0 = silent or ugly \, 3 = love it). Then BREED: the cards are replaced by children of your favourites \, card 1 keeps your best one., f 58;
#X text 430 962 2) Reward one kind of sound for a few generations (say the low growls). The colour patterns grow alike - the population converges - and the mean rating climbs., f 58;
#X text 430 1028 3) Mutation probability 0: crossover only reshuffles rows you already have - nothing new appears. Probability 0.6 with size 1.5: children barely resemble their parents., f 58;
#X text 850 896 4) Rate one card only and breed: every child is a mutated copy of it - a hill-climber (Rojas 17.2.4). Rate two very different cards: many children fall silent - the problem of symmetries., f 58;
#X text 850 962 5) Turn the pitch: the same genome plays from a slow pulse to a whistle. Pitch is not in the genome - rhythm and timbre are., f 58;
#X text 850 1013 6) Keep a sound: print the playing genome. The Pd window shows its 20 numbers \, row by row \, bias last., f 58;
#X connect 224 0 225 0;
#X connect 224 1 225 1;
#X connect 224 0 226 0;
